In the branch $A B,$ both capacitor are arranged in the series combination. Hence, its cquivalent capacitance is given by

\begin{array}{l}
\frac{1}{C_{A B}}=\frac{1}{20}+\frac{1}{20}=\frac{2}{20}=\frac{1}{10} \\
C_{A B}=10 \mu \mathrm{F}
\end{array}

Similarly, in branch $\mathrm{CD}$,

C_{C D}=10 \mu \mathrm{F}

Now, $C_{A B}$ and $C_{C D}$ are connected in parallel combination. Hence, the equivalent capacitance of the circuit,

C_{\mathrm{eq}}=10+10=20 \mu \mathrm{F}

We know that, charge $Q=C_{\mathrm{eq}} V$
Q=20 \times 10^{-6} \times 150 \mathrm{V}

Amount of charge stored $Q=3 \times 10^{-3} C$

We know that, energy stored in the capacitor

=\frac{1}{2} C E^{2}

and energy supplied by the source of emf

=C E^{2}

$\therefore$ Energy dissipated in resistance $R$ = Energy supplied by the source of emf $E$ -  Energy stored in the capacitor

\begin{array}{l}
=C E^{2}-\frac{1}{2} C E^{2} \\
=\left(1-\frac{1}{2}\right) C E^{2}=\frac{1}{2} C E^{2}
\end{array}

According to question the figure can be drawn as below
